In this lesson, we'll look at a really handy shortcut for probability problems called the complement method. Sometimes the question asks for something like "at least one" or "all," and trying to count those cases directly gets messy fast. That's where the complement comes in. The idea is simple. Instead of working out the tricky event, you flip it around and work out the opposite, which is usually just one easy case. From there, you subtract from one to get your answer. So if the question says "at least one head in two coin tosses," instead of listing out head-tail, tail-head, and head-head, you just ask, what's the chance of no heads at all? That's just tail-tail, one case. You find that probability and subtract it from one. The same trick works for "all" situations. If you want the chance that every item passes, the complement is that at least one fails, which is often easier to handle. The recipe is always the same. Identify the event you want, find its opposite, compute its probability, and then take one minus that. It's especially useful for things like rolling two dice and wanting at least one six, or checking whether it rains on at least one day of a seven-day forecast. Once you spot the words "at least one" or "all," think complement. Now let's see it in action.